Under federal law, a baby can be covered through Familienversicherung (free family cover for dependants) in statutory health insurance. SGB V §10 says the children of members of a statutory health insurance fund are insured ("Versichert sind ... die Kinder von Mitgliedern") if all of these conditions are met. The child:

1. has their residence or habitual abode in Germany;
2. is not compulsorily or voluntarily insured in their own right;
3. is not exempt from insurance or released from compulsory insurance;
4. is not self-employed as a main occupation;
5. has no total income that regularly exceeds one seventh of the monthly reference figure.

Children are covered up to their 18th birthday ("bis zur Vollendung des achtzehnten Lebensjahres").

There is one exclusion. A child is not covered this way if the parent who is related to the child and married to, or in a civil partnership with, the member is not a member of a statutory health insurance fund, and that parent's total income regularly exceeds one twelfth of the annual earnings limit ("ein Zwölftel der Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze") and is regularly higher than the member's income.

If the conditions are met through more than one parent, the member chooses the health insurance fund ("wählt das Mitglied die Krankenkasse").

To put the cover in place, the member must report the child to the responsible health insurance fund, with the information needed to run the family cover ("an die zuständige Krankenkasse zu melden"). The member must also report any later changes to that information.

The official family portal says you need the birth certificate for registering with the health insurance fund. It also says you must register the child with the registry office for the place of birth within one week, and that this office issues the birth certificate.

The excerpts do not say what applies if neither parent is a member of a statutory health insurance fund. Whether your child is covered is decided by the health insurance fund of the insured parent, so contact that fund.
